WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:07.210
In the ocean, they have no rival. And on cable,
they have their own week. Our fascination

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:12.040
with sharks has given filmmakers endless opportunities
to prey on audiences' fears, turning this

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.289
oft-misunderstood prehistoric species into
a bloodthirsty killing machine—even if you're

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:21.490
not in the water. Sharks in the desert, sharks
in space, sharks in a tornado, or sharks who

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:25.020
come back from the dead to haunt you in your
backyard...shark movies are now firmly embedded

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.540
as their own subgenre. Let's take a look at
some of our most beloved movies starring Hollywood's

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:30.910
favorite maneater.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:32.149
Shark Night 3D

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:36.859
If you were a teenager in a movie, why would
you ever go to a lake? Nothing good ever happens

00:00:36.859 --> 00:00:40.559
there. That goes double if you're trapped
on an island with no cell service, and your

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:44.949
ex-boyfriend and his buddy fill the water
with sharks so they can film the carnage.

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:48.800
Despite some neat twists, slick production
values for the genre, and Sara Paxton and

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.659
Katherine McPhee in bikinis, Shark Night 3D
was ultimately sunk by a PG-13 rating and

00:00:53.659 --> 00:00:58.149
near comical death scenes. But hey, at least
the dog survives.

00:00:58.149 --> 00:00:58.960
Open Water

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.239
The premise? Pure nightmare fuel. A couple
on a scuba diving vacation are accidentally

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:08.270
left behind (with a lot of sharks) when their
boat crew forgets about them. While the film

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.560
is more of an exercise in psychological horror
and focuses on the broken relationship between

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.570
the two main characters, the constant and
palpable fear that a shark may strike at any

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.539
time makes Open Water one of the most terrifying
shark movies in recent memory. The only happy

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:25.360
ending? It made ten times more than its $500,000
budget.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.659
Deep Blue Sea

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:30.740
If you can ignore a premise that involves
scientists thinking Mako sharks are, like,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:36.310
the best cure for Alzheimer's disease...because
sharks' brains don't age, apparently...you

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:40.960
can appreciate this outrageously fun B-movie.
It's got genetically altered, super-smart

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.200
sharks going HAM—that's "hard as a Mako,
friend"—in an underwater research facility.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.979
And if that weren't enough for our team of
scientists to worry about, the screenwriters

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:55.299
threw in an impending hurricane...just because.
Boasting incredible CGI for its time and Samuel

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:59.259
L. Jackson chewing up all the scenery the
sharks left behind, Deep Blue Sea has earned

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:00.659
cult-classic status.

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:03.119
Plus, you can't beat that LL Cool J rap theme:

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.060
(music playing)

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:10.590
Pure magic.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.260
Bait

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:16.069
You have to at least give the Australian production
Bait a few points for originality. The last

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:20.379
thing you'd expect after a freak tsunami is
sharks swimming down the frozen food section

00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:24.900
at your local grocery store, which by some
miracle still has electricity. Yet here they

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.519
are—and they're the most believable thing
in the movie. You can tell everyone involved

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:33.010
really tried to make something serious here,
but how serious can it be when the hero takes

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:37.129
out a great white with a taser? This would
probably be considered a classic by now if

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:42.140
the filmmakers had been a bit more self-aware
and called it Sharks in a Grocery Store. Chalk

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.549
that up to a missed opportunity. If a group
of mildly annoying survivors trapped in a

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:49.830
supermarket with sharks is your thing, be
sure to add this to your queue.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:50.849
The Reef

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:55.159
Another classic from Australia, 2010's The
Reef proved that you don't need a big budget

00:02:55.159 --> 00:02:59.760
or a convoluted plot to give your audience
legit scares. The tale of five friends who

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.860
set out for Indonesia only to capsize on a
reef is an exercise in hopelessness. Do they

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.870
stay with the boat and risk the very real
possibility of dying of thirst and exposure?

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:15.189
Or attempt swimming to shore in shark-infested
waters? No unnecessary CGI, no cliched tropes,

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:22.680
just people in the water…slowly being eaten
alive, one by one. Delicious.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.930
Soul Surfer

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.189
Real-life events can be difficult to dramatize,
but the true story of surfer Bethany Hamilton

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:33.090
getting her left arm ripped off by a tiger
shark, and her subsequent struggle to surf

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:38.790
again, was a story made for celluloid. Based
on Hamilton's 2004 autobiography Soul Surfer:

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.650
A True Story of Faith, Family, and Fighting
to Get Back on the Board, it didn't blow critics

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:47.489
away. But found a home among audiences who
like their shark attacks with an inspirational

00:03:47.489 --> 00:03:52.269
message of hope. Of course, for the audiences
who have no need for dumb things like hope

00:03:52.269 --> 00:03:54.849
or inspiration, there's...

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:55.709
Sharknado

00:03:55.709 --> 00:04:02.689
Syfy's Sharknado knows full well that it's
a movie about, well, a shark tornado, and

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:07.170
it embraces that insane premise with every
frame. In between the actors winking at the

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:11.349
camera, Sharknado gives you some of the most
outrageous and over-the-top sharks to ever

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:15.780
hit the big screen. Running down the B-movie
checklist (and marking off many items more

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:20.889
than once), this movie can even be considered
a work of art considering the glorious lengths

00:04:20.889 --> 00:04:26.000
it takes to not take itself seriously. The
dialogue is almost as bad as the color correction,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.830
the character development is more unbelievable
than the plot, and the CGI is almost as painful

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:36.410
as the one-liners. But you know what? It works.
It delivers in every way possible. If you

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.169
don't like Sharknado, you don't like fun.

00:04:39.169 --> 00:04:40.930
Blue Water, White Death

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.460
The only documentary on our list, Blue Water,
White Death follows filmmaker and underwater

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.690
photojournalist Peter Gimbel's arduous nine-month
trek across the globe to capture a great white

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:55.400
shark on film for the very first time. And
where did he find one? Right where he probably

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:00.509
shoulda started: South Australia. With spellbinding
footage, Gimbel shows us the legendary great

00:05:00.509 --> 00:05:05.180
white in its full (and terrifying) glory.
Fun fact: Crew members Ron and Valerie Taylor

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.280
provided Steven Spielberg with great white
footage for Jaws. Speaking of which...

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.509
Jaws

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:15.100
The opening sequence of Steven Spielberg's
Jaws boasts the most terrifying shark attack

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:20.720
ever filmed…and you never even see the shark.
The sense of building dread as you watch Police

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.690
Chief Brody try—and repeatedly fail—to
convince officials in his coastal town to

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:29.850
close the beach during peak tourist season
is enough to make you never want to set foot

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:34.050
in an ocean again. John Williams' iconic score
will make you pause before entering a pool.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.910
We won't even mention the classic lines, because
you know them all.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:39.639
Okay, maybe just one…

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:43.169
"You're gonna need a bigger boat."

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:47.090
So good. Of course, a movie this good just
has to have a sequel, right?

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:48.069
Jaws 2

00:05:48.069 --> 00:05:53.020
"Just when you thought it was safe to go back
in the water"

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:56.770
...now that's a tagline for a sequel. A sequel,
we might add, that Roy Scheider only agreed

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:01.210
to star in to get out from under his contract
at Universal. It's debatable whether even

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:05.639
Spielberg could have saved the script for
Jaws 2. And you'd think Amity's mayor would

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:09.849
be more inclined to listen to Police Chief
Brody when he cries "shark!" this time around.

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:14.180
But despite not coming close to the original,
Jaws 2 does offer up some scary moments. As

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:19.759
well as perhaps the greatest shark electrocution
death scene in movie history:

00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:23.169
"Watch out!"

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:32.409
"Come on! Come on! Open wide! Open wide! Say
aahhhh!"

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:45.539
Well...not that there are too many shark electrocution
death scenes to choose from, but you get the

00:06:45.539 --> 00:06:46.060
idea.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:50.810
Seriously, though, no matter how many bad
sequels it spawned, the original Jaws is without

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:56.509
a doubt the greatest shark movie of all time—past,
present, and future. Maybe it's time we stop

00:06:56.509 --> 00:06:59.770
making them. After we get Sharknado 4, of
course.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:03.780
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:07.210
just saw. Leave us a comment to let us know
which shark movie was your favorite...

